1
00:00:14,611 --> 00:00:15,451
Greetings.

2
00:00:16,292 --> 00:00:18,015
I trust you've had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:18,295 --> 00:00:21,620
I do not say happy, though I hope it has been happy.

4
00:00:22,841 --> 00:00:30,332
Namotasa Bhagavato Harahato Sambha Sambhodasa.

5
00:00:31,213 --> 00:00:39,505
Namotasa Bhagavato Harahato Sambha Sambhodasa.

6
00:00:40,075 --> 00:00:48,646
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhassa.

7
00:00:49,267 --> 00:00:53,612
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed noble and fully self-awakened one.

8
00:00:56,457 --> 00:01:02,365
Put my special industry-level lapel microphone on.

9
00:01:02,886 --> 00:01:03,365
There we are.

10
00:01:05,248 --> 00:01:07,010
I wanted to...

11
00:01:08,070 --> 00:01:15,799
just carry on with this not-self business, just to make it even clearer for those people who feel baffled by it.

12
00:01:17,299 --> 00:01:19,382
I have had some questions sent me.

13
00:01:19,983 --> 00:01:35,378
I hope the questioners will be patient enough to wait a couple of days, because tomorrow I want to actually go through the core discourse on not-self, which is the second one traditionally that he gave to his five companions.

14
00:01:36,438 --> 00:01:41,963
So it's a case of recognizing what the Buddha means when he talks about self.

15
00:01:46,147 --> 00:01:54,335
It's translating the word atta in Pali or the language of that time.

16
00:01:55,417 --> 00:02:01,162
And it's still there, of course, atta in Hinduism, the atta is your eternal soul, whatever.

17
00:02:02,358 --> 00:02:10,730
So now is to do if we forget the word self for a minute and just talk about identity, identity.

18
00:02:11,385 --> 00:02:12,788
What do you identify with?

19
00:02:13,028 --> 00:02:14,812
Now, we have all sorts of identities.

20
00:02:15,693 --> 00:02:22,564
You know, I'm a father, a son, husband, wife, brother, daughter, workmate, etc., etc.

21
00:02:23,326 --> 00:02:28,795
I also have social relationships, which I identify with.

22
00:02:29,657 --> 00:02:35,587
My job, you know, I am a worker, I'm an office worker, etc., etc.

23
00:02:35,719 --> 00:02:39,806
But what we're talking about is your existential identity.

24
00:02:39,925 --> 00:02:41,668
What are you in the face of death?

25
00:02:42,169 --> 00:02:45,913
That's where the answer, that's what we're trying to discover or find out.

26
00:02:46,735 --> 00:02:50,139
And there are two major opposite opinions.

27
00:02:50,460 --> 00:03:03,076
One is that I exist, meaning me, this that I think I am, exists after death in some way, and me, what I think I am,

28
00:03:03,259 --> 00:03:30,870
completely annihilates that death so there's there's your two opposites right now in between that um you might find people who say i am the body um and also have some idea of afterlife i was with a friend down in cornwall he's died unfortunately i used to go down there and spend some time with him and uh he was living with his mother and father so we're talking about 10 years ago so and um

29
00:03:31,153 --> 00:03:42,006
We were having a discussion, we were sat on the couch, and his mother and father sat on these separate chairs, and of course the TV was on, and we were having a discussion about something.

30
00:03:42,026 --> 00:03:45,250
And his father was seemingly listening to what was on the television.

31
00:03:45,911 --> 00:03:52,098
Anyway, at some point in our supposedly private, quiet discussion, I said to him, well, we're not the body.

32
00:03:52,437 --> 00:03:58,224
And his father suddenly burst into this declaration, yes, we are, you see.

33
00:04:00,145 --> 00:04:10,375
And it was such a loud snap that silence and embarrassment descended upon the four of us for a little while before we continued our discussion.

34
00:04:11,937 --> 00:04:15,640
So ironically, of course, he died that very year.

35
00:04:16,541 --> 00:04:19,545
I don't know what his last thoughts were.

36
00:04:20,505 --> 00:04:25,069
So there are some people who believe in some way that they are the body.

37
00:04:25,149 --> 00:04:26,911
They are definitely the body.

38
00:04:26,891 --> 00:04:48,461
the most subtle one of course is that no I'm not the body that disappears in death I am my heart mind the old word for that is so as I work for it anima would be the Latin psyche would be the no psycho psychology and all that psyche would be the Greek for it so you have body heart and

39
00:04:48,812 --> 00:04:51,995
heart-mind, and then usually have spirit.

40
00:04:52,456 --> 00:04:53,918
And that's what most people think.

41
00:04:54,218 --> 00:04:59,103
Well, I'm not the body, I'm not the heart-mind with its emotions and thoughts.

42
00:04:59,625 --> 00:05:01,125
I am the one who knows.

43
00:05:01,427 --> 00:05:02,447
I am consciousness.

44
00:05:03,088 --> 00:05:07,593
So that's your more subtle position of those who think about afterlife.

45
00:05:07,612 --> 00:05:10,036
Now the Buddha says, we're not any of that.

46
00:05:10,475 --> 00:05:11,216
See, that's the point.

47
00:05:11,937 --> 00:05:15,822
And to put it in his categories, he would talk about the five khandhas.

48
00:05:16,041 --> 00:05:18,685
So there's the body, that's the rupa,

49
00:05:19,137 --> 00:05:26,783
And the nāma, the mind, includes all your feelings, both physical, mental, pleasant, unpleasant, etc.

50
00:05:27,444 --> 00:05:35,451
All your perceptions, your thoughts, your images in the mind, which produces your imagination and thinking.

51
00:05:36,252 --> 00:05:47,423
And then you have this section of sankāra, which is translated as volitional formations, because this is where we create things, create with the will, volition.

52
00:05:48,836 --> 00:05:51,884
But I most prefer, just a very simple word, our habits.

53
00:05:52,305 --> 00:05:54,529
It's just the way we perform.

54
00:05:54,850 --> 00:06:00,805
And that's what we are, just a compendium of habits, just a group of habits.

55
00:06:01,257 --> 00:06:02,298
and consciousness.

56
00:06:03,139 --> 00:06:22,836
Now consciousness, vijnana, is variously used, so it's quite difficult to find out what this consciousness is when it comes to the khanda itself, the actual five heaps or aggregates that we're made of.

57
00:06:24,577 --> 00:06:29,543
But the one thing that points to what it is, is the point of contact.

58
00:06:30,754 --> 00:06:35,922
Now the point of contact, you must have an object which is coming into the mind.

59
00:06:36,362 --> 00:06:38,305
So you're looking at something like a tree.

60
00:06:39,326 --> 00:06:44,411
And there must be a sense base that is bringing the information in, which is the eye.

61
00:06:45,192 --> 00:06:50,620
And there's consciousness, which of course makes it bright and you become aware of it.

62
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:51,841
I've got to be careful here.

63
00:06:52,223 --> 00:07:01,898
So consciousness, at the point of contact, is just a screen upon which the object and the sense base work to create something on a screen.

64
00:07:02,238 --> 00:07:09,411
That's all it is, a screen, something that allows something to appear, right?

65
00:07:10,192 --> 00:07:13,317
Now, the...

66
00:07:14,242 --> 00:07:21,170
The problem is that this consciousness is arising and passing away with every bit of information that comes in.

67
00:07:21,449 --> 00:07:28,557
And you could say bits, you know, just like we say in computers, you know, bits and megabytes and all that, these are bytes.

68
00:07:28,879 --> 00:07:37,749
These are just little, tiny little bytes that come in and the mind works on it and produces an image which then comes on the screen.

69
00:07:38,709 --> 00:07:38,949
Okay?

70
00:07:39,790 --> 00:07:42,274
Now, the Buddha says we're not any of that.

71
00:07:42,557 --> 00:07:44,360
The Buddha says, well, not any of that.

72
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:48,629
He says there is something within all that which is confused with it.

73
00:07:49,028 --> 00:07:51,033
And that's where the delusion lies.

74
00:07:51,394 --> 00:08:03,435
And at the most subtle point of delusion, it is the confusion of this awareness, this Buddha within, this intuitive awareness with that screen.

75
00:08:03,415 --> 00:08:08,482
So that would be the same as, for instance, when we absorb into a film.

76
00:08:08,742 --> 00:08:13,208
So if you're in a cinema or watching it on a screen, you absorb into the film.

77
00:08:13,288 --> 00:08:16,713
And for a while you live in that imaginative world.

78
00:08:18,274 --> 00:08:21,759
And during that time, there's not you or the world or anything.

79
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:23,161
You're completely absorbed.

80
00:08:24,442 --> 00:08:31,398
So generally speaking, we're absorbed in the world that is appearing on this screen.

81
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:42,484
So now, having said all that, if you think about these words that are used here, you see, which sound very confusing.

82
00:08:42,464 --> 00:08:46,774
So things like the self exists for me.

83
00:08:47,416 --> 00:08:49,280
No self exists for me.

84
00:08:49,761 --> 00:08:53,471
So that means he doesn't identify with anything or put it this way.

85
00:08:53,510 --> 00:08:54,352
Nothing is it.

86
00:08:54,373 --> 00:08:58,683
Remember, self here means identity, permanent identity.

87
00:08:59,068 --> 00:09:02,134
So there's no permanent identity existing.

88
00:09:02,153 --> 00:09:04,518
I perceive self with self.

89
00:09:04,638 --> 00:09:10,226
So I perceive that I am self-awareness and the body and mind.

90
00:09:11,308 --> 00:09:17,337
The two things that they perceive self, so they think both sides are self.

91
00:09:17,357 --> 00:09:20,823
And I perceive not self with the self is that the observer perceives

92
00:09:21,344 --> 00:09:36,365
is the only real, and the body and mind are what it experiences So the not-self here is the body and mind, the experience that we're having And the true self, the true identity, is with the observer

93
00:09:36,346 --> 00:09:39,308
And then you have, I perceive self with not-self.

94
00:09:39,589 --> 00:09:46,835
So here, it's the perceived that is, the perceiver is not the self, but what is actually perceived.

95
00:09:47,195 --> 00:09:54,902
So that's this business of the consciousness being an emergent property.

96
00:09:56,543 --> 00:09:59,346
And then finally, there's a full-blown sense of self.

97
00:09:59,405 --> 00:10:05,390
It is the self of mind that speaks and feels and experiences here and there, the result of good and bad actions.

98
00:10:06,046 --> 00:10:15,982
And this self of mine, you see, this identity I have is permanent, everlasting, eternal, not subject to change, and it endures as long as eternity.

99
00:10:16,283 --> 00:10:24,476
So that's obviously the self as agent, the feeler, everything you experience now, that's what you are, and that will last forever.

100
00:10:25,063 --> 00:10:40,863
All that the Buddha said, and I love to repeat these things to myself, that is, this speculative view is called a thicket of views, a wilderness of views, a contortion of views, a vacillation of views, the fetter of views.

101
00:10:41,583 --> 00:10:51,677
So fettered by the fetter of views, the untaught ordinary person is not freed from birth, aging, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief, and despair.

102
00:10:52,216 --> 00:10:54,480
He is not freed from suffering, I say.

103
00:10:56,062 --> 00:11:02,590
So I don't know whether that helps to clarify or makes things even more complicated.

104
00:11:03,071 --> 00:11:05,594
But I would love now to, let's see what time it is.

105
00:11:05,734 --> 00:11:07,216
Oh yes, got plenty of time.

106
00:11:07,235 --> 00:11:12,721
I want to read the story of, where's he gone?

107
00:11:17,788 --> 00:11:18,369
Have I lost him?

108
00:11:18,749 --> 00:11:19,590
Oh dear, dear, dear.

109
00:11:24,328 --> 00:11:24,690
Oh, yeah.

110
00:11:25,270 --> 00:11:32,865
So this is the opening paragraphs of the Greater Discourse on the Destruction of Craving.

111
00:11:33,287 --> 00:11:34,067
That's what it's about.

112
00:11:34,089 --> 00:11:36,994
You see the Mahātāṅha Sankhaya Sutta, right?

113
00:11:37,475 --> 00:11:39,840
And it's number 38 in the Majjhima Nikaya.

114
00:11:40,293 --> 00:11:43,296
That's my main textbook from the Jiminy guy.

115
00:11:43,816 --> 00:11:47,860
He's upside down, isn't he?

116
00:11:47,879 --> 00:11:52,703
If you want to follow me, so this is number 38, The Great Discourse and the Destruction of Craving.

117
00:11:53,365 --> 00:11:55,246
So this is the story of Sati.

118
00:11:55,807 --> 00:12:00,711
Now, thus have I heard, on one occasion, the Blessed One was living at Savity in Jeta's Grove.

119
00:12:00,731 --> 00:12:07,498
So remember, that's probably his most favorite monastery built by an art appendica, the businessman.

120
00:12:08,203 --> 00:12:13,950
Now, on that occasion, a pernicious view had arisen in a bhikkhu called Sati, the son of a fisherman.

121
00:12:14,649 --> 00:12:24,860
Thus, as I understand the Dharma taught by the Blessed One, it is this same consciousness that runs and wanders through the round of rebirths, not another.

122
00:12:25,701 --> 00:12:33,028
So he's saying that this consciousness is the consciousness, remember, not the body-mind, not the, yeah, not the body-mind.

123
00:12:33,347 --> 00:12:36,532
It's the consciousness that is the same from birth to birth.

124
00:12:36,552 --> 00:12:38,092
It just runs through all rebirths.

125
00:12:38,562 --> 00:12:45,807
Now, several Bhikkhus, having heard about this, went to the Bhikkhusati and asked him, friend, is it true that such a pernicious view has arisen?

126
00:12:46,729 --> 00:12:47,870
Exactly so, friends.

127
00:12:47,909 --> 00:12:48,331
That's funny.

128
00:12:48,350 --> 00:12:50,993
He seems to agree that it is a pernicious view.

129
00:12:51,813 --> 00:12:59,639
As I understand the Dharma taught by the Blessed One, it is the same consciousness that runs and wanders through the rounds of rebirth and not another.

130
00:13:00,581 --> 00:13:07,067
Then those Bhikkhus, desiring to detach him from this pernicious view, pressed and questioned and questioned him.

131
00:13:08,043 --> 00:13:10,106
Friends, sati, do not say so.

132
00:13:10,187 --> 00:13:12,611
Do not misrepresent the Blessed One.

133
00:13:12,631 --> 00:13:14,975
It is not good to misrepresent the Blessed One.

134
00:13:15,297 --> 00:13:17,461
The Blessed One would not speak thus.

135
00:13:18,022 --> 00:13:31,586
But in many discourses, the Blessed One states consciousness to be dependently arisen since without condition there is no origination of consciousness.

136
00:13:33,153 --> 00:13:45,558
And although pressed and questioned and crossed examined by those Bhikkhus in this way, the Bhikkhusati, son of a fisherman, was still obstinately adhered to that pernicious view and continued to insist upon it.

137
00:13:46,601 --> 00:14:08,525
Since the Bhikkhus were unable to detach him from this pernicious view, they went to the Blessed One and after paying homage to him, they sat to one side and told him what had occurred, adding, "'Venerable Sir, since we could not detach the Bhikkhusati, the son of a fisherman, from this pernicious view, we have reported this matter to the Blessed One.'

138
00:14:08,759 --> 00:14:18,654
Then the Blessed One addressed the certain Bhikkhu thus, Come Bhikkhu, tell the Bhikkhu Sati, son of a fisherman, in my name, that the teacher calls him.

139
00:14:18,674 --> 00:14:20,118
Yes, Venerable Sir, he replied.

140
00:14:20,357 --> 00:14:26,086
And he went to Bhikkhu Sati and told him, Sati, the teacher calls you.

141
00:14:27,308 --> 00:14:29,011
Friend Sati, friend Sati.

142
00:14:30,292 --> 00:14:35,523
Yes, friend, he replied, and he went to the Blessed One, and after paying homage to him, sat down at one side.

143
00:14:35,602 --> 00:14:41,535
And the Blessed One then asked him, Sati, is it true that the following pernicious view has arisen in you?

144
00:14:42,498 --> 00:14:50,916
As I understand the Dharma taught by the Blessed One, it is the same consciousness that runs and wanders through the rounds of rebirth and not another.

145
00:14:51,924 --> 00:15:01,696
Exactly so, Venerable Sir, as I understand the Dharma taught by the Blessed One, it is the same consciousness that runs and wanders through the rounds of rebirth and not another.

146
00:15:02,937 --> 00:15:04,840
What is that consciousness, Sati?

147
00:15:05,701 --> 00:15:12,368
Venerable Sir, it is that which speaks and feels and experiences here and now the result of good and bad actions.

148
00:15:13,109 --> 00:15:14,471
Misguided man!

149
00:15:14,990 --> 00:15:18,355
To whom have you ever known me to teach the Dharma in that way?

150
00:15:18,875 --> 00:15:31,011
Misguided man, in many discourses have I stated consciousness to be dependently arisen, since without condition there is no origination of consciousness.

151
00:15:31,032 --> 00:15:43,330
But you, misguided man, have misrepresented us by your wrong grasp and injured yourself and stored up much demerit, for this will lead to your harm and suffering for a long time.

152
00:15:44,322 --> 00:15:48,087
Then the Blessed One addressed the Bhikkhus thus, Bhikkhus, what do you think?

153
00:15:48,970 --> 00:15:57,363
Has this Bhikkhus Sati, son of a fisherman, kindled even a spark of wisdom in this Dharma, in this discipline?

154
00:15:58,304 --> 00:15:59,886
How could he, Venerable Sir?

155
00:16:00,248 --> 00:16:01,470
No, Venerable Sir.

156
00:16:02,041 --> 00:16:16,426
And this was said, Bhikkhusati, son of a fisherman, sat silent, dismayed, with shoulders drooping and head down, glum and without response.

157
00:16:17,989 --> 00:16:24,298
Then knowing this, the Blessed One said, misguided man, you will be recognized by your own pernicious view.

158
00:16:24,961 --> 00:16:27,524
I shall question the Bhikkhus on this matter.

159
00:16:28,432 --> 00:16:45,094
Then the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus, Bhikkhus, do you understand the Dhamma taught by me that this bhikkhu, the son of a fisherman, does when he misrepresents us by his wrong grasp and injures himself and stores up such demerit?

160
00:16:45,754 --> 00:16:56,027
No, Venerable Sir, for in many discourses the Blessed One has stated consciousness to be dependently arisen since without condition

161
00:16:56,006 --> 00:17:00,030
there is no origination, there is no arising of consciousness.

162
00:17:01,212 --> 00:17:19,752
Good bhikkhus, it is good that you understand the Dhamma taught by me thus, for in many discourses I have stated consciousness to be dependently arisen, since without condition there is no origination or arising of consciousness.

163
00:17:20,509 --> 00:17:35,448
But this Bhikkhu Sati, son of a fisherman, misrepresents us by his wrong grasp and injures himself and stores up much demerit, for this will lead to his harm and suffering for a long time."

164
00:17:37,230 --> 00:17:41,255
Now, you have to have a certain sympathy for poor old Sati.

165
00:17:41,654 --> 00:17:49,825
I mean, he sat there, silent, sad, dismayed, shoulders drooping, head down, glum and without response.

166
00:17:49,805 --> 00:17:51,186
Well, you'd want to hug him, wouldn't you?

167
00:17:51,207 --> 00:17:52,769
I mean, you know, poor old Satie.

168
00:17:53,250 --> 00:17:59,700
And it keeps going on about him being a son of a fisherman, but they all have that sort of name behind them.

169
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:09,335
I put a little twist on it to suggest a bit of class prejudice, son of a fisherman.

170
00:18:09,915 --> 00:18:12,640
And that's, of course, how these stories grow, you see.

171
00:18:12,788 --> 00:18:20,622
So the next one, it would be, if I were retelling this tale elsewhere, I'd say, but the son of a fisherman, something like that, you see.

172
00:18:21,242 --> 00:18:28,272
So you have to be careful with these stories that obviously played upon over generations, really.

173
00:18:28,292 --> 00:18:32,999
500 years they were handed down from person to person.

174
00:18:33,339 --> 00:18:34,361
So they obviously changed.

175
00:18:34,601 --> 00:18:48,921
But I think we can take the root thing that there was a monk called Sati, who was the son of a fisherman, who got it wrong and made this mistake of thinking that the Buddha was talking about consciousness.

176
00:18:49,288 --> 00:18:54,315
when he said that there was something eternal in us, something that doesn't die, etc.

177
00:18:56,037 --> 00:19:07,410
And when you read the discourses like that, I have the patience to just read them through, then of course the core of the teaching keeps coming back at you.

178
00:19:07,810 --> 00:19:16,461
And the core of the teaching is obviously consciousness is dependently arisen since without condition,

179
00:19:17,722 --> 00:19:24,951
There is no arising origination of consciousness.

180
00:19:25,613 --> 00:19:25,874
Right?

181
00:19:26,193 --> 00:19:27,134
So that's telling us.

182
00:19:28,176 --> 00:19:38,730
Now, unfortunately, then the Buddha goes on about consciousness and dependent origination and shows us how we can come to an end of craving.

183
00:19:39,330 --> 00:19:46,059
But he hasn't let go of sati because right at the end of the discourse, the conclusion, bhikkhus, remember this deliverance.

184
00:19:46,039 --> 00:19:48,844
through the destruction of craving, right?

185
00:19:48,904 --> 00:19:54,113
So he's been talking about how to destroy craving as taught in brief by me.

186
00:19:54,854 --> 00:20:02,586
But this Bhikkhusati, son of a fisherman, is caught up in a vast net of craving and a trammel of craving.

187
00:20:03,468 --> 00:20:04,368
Poor, poor Sati.

188
00:20:04,388 --> 00:20:06,192
He must have sat through all this.

189
00:20:08,685 --> 00:20:14,782
That is what the Blessed One said, and the Bickels were satisfied and delighted in the Blessed One's words.

190
00:20:15,865 --> 00:20:20,198
So I think that gets the message home.

191
00:20:22,050 --> 00:20:41,442
so uh tomorrow what i mean to do is actually look at the um the core discourse or the main discourse on not self which which is traditionally the second one that he gave to the five um companions when we left remember and returned when he was fully awakened so i'll be doing that

